WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.300
You are safe behind your shields,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.792
and the orcs are going
to impale themselves

00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:08.810
on your pointy sticks.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:10.561
And then the elves just decide,

00:00:10.561 --> 00:00:12.665
no, instead we'll jump in front of that

00:00:12.665 --> 00:00:14.999
and put ourselves needlessly into danger.

00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:16.358
My name is Roel Konijnendijk.

00:00:16.358 --> 00:00:18.626
I teach ancient history at
the University of Edinburgh.

00:00:18.626 --> 00:00:20.300
You might remember me from part one.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.064
Today, we'll be looking
at ancient-warfare scenes

00:00:22.064 --> 00:00:24.988
in modern movies and TV and
seeing how real they are.

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:27.786
Nock!

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:30.138
Draw!

00:00:31.204 --> 00:00:32.074
Loose!

00:00:32.074 --> 00:00:33.299
Soldiers: Loose!

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.830
All these movies do this.

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:37.630
They all have these archers lined up.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:38.827
They give these sequences,

00:00:38.827 --> 00:00:41.860
"nock, draw, loose," over and over again,

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.520
but this never happened.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.900
You don't order them to shoot in volleys.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.690
When you order everyone
to draw at the same time,

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.290
some of these guys are
going to get really tired,

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.650
and they're not going to be effective.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.483
Others are going to be like,

00:00:51.483 --> 00:00:52.440
this is too slow for me.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.920
I can shoot faster than this.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.128
There were no orders to
archers to fire in volleys.

00:00:57.128 --> 00:00:58.681
[shields dropping]

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.090
The coordination required to do this

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:05.400
is completely impossible.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.900
There were battle formations

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.990
where you would have shield walls in front

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:10.402
and then spears sticking out

00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:11.839
to sort of drive the enemy back.

00:01:11.839 --> 00:01:13.440
Obviously the Macedonian pikemen

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:14.570
are the big famous example.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.710
There are Mesopotamian examples

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.760
from way back, 2000 BC.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.280
It wouldn't work like this, though.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.710
You couldn't really form them in a circle.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:22.920
They would form up in blocks,

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.380
and they would maneuver in blocks.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.612
And that's hard enough.

00:01:25.612 --> 00:01:28.195
[men chanting]

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.500
Once you're fully surrounded,

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.420
I mean, there's nothing
you can do, really.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:36.253
You're in big trouble.

00:01:36.253 --> 00:01:37.710
You can try to break the spears.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.650
Persians are known to do
that against the Greeks.

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.130
Their spears are longer, so they literally

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.223
went up and snapped them in half by hand,

00:01:43.223 --> 00:01:44.500
but that's a desperation move,

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.070
and it doesn't really get you anywhere.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.780
If you're in an encirclement like that,

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.265
of a proper pike formation,

00:01:49.265 --> 00:01:52.373
then you're just doomed.
You probably surrender.

00:01:55.848 --> 00:01:58.348
[gasping]

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.140
Crushes in battle do happen.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.638
But most of the time when
people do get crushed

00:02:04.638 --> 00:02:07.220
into a small space and
the dead start piling up,

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:08.860
it's because they're trying to run away

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:10.567
and they can't escape,
because there's a river

00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:13.568
or a ravine or a wall,
and they get stuck there.

00:02:13.568 --> 00:02:14.870
The panic of a trample,

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.665
they do depict that pretty well.

00:02:16.665 --> 00:02:19.248
[men shouting]

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:23.230
When horses turn up unexpectedly,

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.100
especially behind the infantry line,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.470
you would give up and run.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:27.670
There is almost no chance that

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:28.800
that formation would hold.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.460
The very unrealistic thing in this scene

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.400
is that no one knew
that cavalry was there.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.720
It's possible to hide, you know,

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.970
small units in woods or hollow roads

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:38.800
or behind some geographical feature.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.020
But a large force of cavalry

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.340
is going to throw up a lot of dust.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:42.590
It's going to make a lot of noise.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:43.960
It's going to, you know, scare the birds

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:45.501
and the animals. You know this is coming.

00:02:45.501 --> 00:02:47.680
I think this one does an interesting job

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.695
of trying to give you the sort of

00:02:49.695 --> 00:02:51.295
visceral horror of a battle.

00:02:51.295 --> 00:02:52.440
And I really do appreciate that.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.748
The tactics are nonsense,

00:02:53.748 --> 00:02:55.804
but it does give a sense of

00:02:55.804 --> 00:02:57.453
desperation of some of these struggles.

00:02:57.453 --> 00:02:58.765
So maybe 5 out of 10,

00:02:58.765 --> 00:02:59.929
6 out of 10.

00:02:59.929 --> 00:03:02.596
[horn sounding]

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.320
That big horn is supposed
to be like a morale boost.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:09.520
And in that sense, it can work.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.702
A lot of troops before battle

00:03:10.702 --> 00:03:12.270
would try and sing songs together,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.130
just like sports teams
might do, to hype them up.

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:17.330
So that kind of thing
would work for a horn.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.807
That shouldn't be confused with the idea

00:03:18.807 --> 00:03:20.630
of using it to give orders.

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:21.930
That's a separate thing.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.050
The Romans were very good at that.

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:25.370
They had a number of
different trumpet signals,

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:26.532
and the troops all knew, like,

00:03:26.532 --> 00:03:28.290
if you hear this signal,
if you hear that signal,

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.600
you have to do different things.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:30.750
But for instance, the Greeks, I mean,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:32.022
they had one big trumpet.

00:03:32.022 --> 00:03:33.590
When it sounded, you charge.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:36.256
If it sounds again,
you retreat. That's it.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.348
Why would you do that? [laughs]

00:03:47.348 --> 00:03:49.120
Like, why would they try to get

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.330
the cavalry to slide over the shield wall

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:52.820
into their own formation?

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:55.100
You want the cavalry to
stop in front of you,

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.510
because at the moment the cavalry stops

00:03:57.510 --> 00:03:59.780
and is stuck in front of your spear wall,

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:01.060
they lose all their advantages.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:02.150
They're not mobile anymore.

00:04:02.150 --> 00:04:03.740
They don't have that momentum anymore.

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:06.425
The elves clearly have a
very effective spear wall,

00:04:06.425 --> 00:04:08.320
and then they just duck under it

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:09.870
and let the cavalry slide over it.

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:12.930
What? [laughs]

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:13.763
I don't know why.

00:04:13.763 --> 00:04:14.879
I don't know what they were doing.

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:22.670
Both the elves and the dwarves in this one

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:25.640
have this double-layered shield wall.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.200
There are battle formations
where you would do that,

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:29.836
where you would try to stop cavalry

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:31.553
by building these
double-layered shield walls.

00:04:31.553 --> 00:04:33.860
This is based on a late
Roman infantry formation,

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.535
where you'd put your infantry
close together at the front

00:04:36.535 --> 00:04:38.471
and double shield wall or
even triple shield wall.

00:04:38.471 --> 00:04:41.554
But in most battles, it's
such a static formation,

00:04:41.554 --> 00:04:43.300
it removes all your opportunities

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.110
to maneuver or to move.

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:46.270
You wouldn't want to do that

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:47.696
unless you were absolutely sure

00:04:47.696 --> 00:04:49.110
what the enemy was going to do

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.370
was going to be running straight into you.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.730
If there was anything else they might do,

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.170
you want to keep your flexibility

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:55.960
by keeping your lines separated

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.760
and just rely on a single wall of shields,

00:04:57.760 --> 00:04:59.635
because that will do most of the time.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.170
If you've got a static defense,

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:08.720
you have your spear wall,

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.450
the orcs are charging straight into it

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:12.130
with no sense of self-preservation,

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:13.970
that's exactly what you
want them to do, right?

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:15.440
You are safe behind your shields,

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:16.990
and the orcs are going
to impale themselves

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:18.220
on your pointy sticks.

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:19.283
All you wanted to have

00:05:19.283 --> 00:05:21.550
is for that to be exactly like that

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:23.280
until the orcs stop coming.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.025
And then the elves just decide,

00:05:25.025 --> 00:05:27.180
no, instead we'll jump in front of that

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.730
and put ourselves needlessly into danger

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.650
so that we can fight
these orcs hand to hand.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:33.768
It makes no sense. It serves no purpose.

00:05:33.768 --> 00:05:36.351
[shouting]

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:42.640
Wedge formations are debated

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:43.960
because, on the one hand,

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.250
we have a lot of sources saying that

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:47.560
wedge formations existed,
mainly for cavalry,

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.080
but it's not entirely certain
how that would even work.

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:51.230
Because most of what you're doing

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:52.640
is just putting one guy at the front

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:53.763
who's going to be exposed

00:05:53.763 --> 00:05:55.988
to everyone in the enemy line.

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:57.570
So unless you've already made a hole,

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.810
that wedge is not going
to give you any advantage.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:00.960
It's just actually going to put you

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.240
in a really dangerous position

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.552
where you're feeding your men

00:06:03.552 --> 00:06:05.243
one at a time into the enemy.

00:06:05.243 --> 00:06:07.073
With cavalry, it's not
clear whether it even works.

00:06:07.073 --> 00:06:09.550
It's showing you some interesting
heavy-infantry formations,

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.517
but otherwise, like, 4 out of 10.

00:06:16.608 --> 00:06:19.500
So, this is Kubrick trying to visualize

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.690
a real Roman battle formation,

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:26.210
which is this three
lines in a checkerboard,

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:27.610
which, going into battle,

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:30.844
they would actually try
and form some kind of line.

00:06:30.844 --> 00:06:33.040
We're not actually sure how this worked.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.853
It's also possible that
they actually fought

00:06:34.853 --> 00:06:37.152
in these checkerboards,
which would give them room

00:06:37.152 --> 00:06:39.818
to sort of withdraw between
the ranks of the others

00:06:39.818 --> 00:06:41.440
and sort of maneuver in and out.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.930
When the front was getting tired

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:44.650
or when it was suffering
too many casualties

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:46.080
and when it couldn't get through,

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.210
you'd just pull them back
and send in the second line,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.490
which would consist of
more experienced troops.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.090
And then they would have a go.

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:54.320
This is just an enormous mob of guys,

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:57.270
much too deep to serve any purpose.

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.012
Like, get in formation!

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.600
Oh, no, it landed on him!

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.850
[laughs]

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:11.572
Like, it bounced over the one

00:07:11.572 --> 00:07:12.823
and landed on the other guy.

00:07:12.823 --> 00:07:14.434
Oh, I hope he got his paycheck.

00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:15.820
[laughs]

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:19.090
Killed by a massive flaming corn dog.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:20.420
Excellent work.

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:21.790
Hollywood just likes fire.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:23.410
They just want to have
fire in their battles.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:24.890
They want to throw big balls of fire,

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:26.320
and they want to have rollers of fire

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:28.200
and balls of fire to disrupt formations.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:29.790
It seems to work in theory,

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:33.265
because you want these
orderly formations disrupted.

00:07:33.265 --> 00:07:34.893
In that sense, it makes tactical sense.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.610
The casualty rate of these battles

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.220
in Hollywood is ridiculous.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.000
The result is always that everyone dies.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.760
This is just not how battles work.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:51.910
You have a certain number
of men in a formation,

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:52.960
and when they start to see

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:54.430
that things are going against them,

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:56.983
or especially when they
realize they've lost order,

00:07:56.983 --> 00:07:57.870
they're going to retreat.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.240
They're going to get out of there,

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.270
and then they'll see what happens next.

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:02.630
Or if the enemy is running away

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:03.958
and they're being chased by cavalry,

00:08:03.958 --> 00:08:06.800
that's when you see the
real casualties pile up.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:08.450
But in these formation battles,

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:10.460
most of the time casualties
would be quite low.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:12.735
6 out of 10 for trying.

00:08:12.735 --> 00:08:14.270
It must've been an enormous effort

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:17.170
to try and get all these
extras trained to do this.

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:19.637
And then they get rolled
over by a flaming corn dog.

00:08:19.637 --> 00:08:20.660
That's a missed opportunity.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:22.070
That poor guy. I hope he's well.

00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:22.980
I hope he's still around.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.178
Hold the ranks! Hold the ranks!

00:08:25.178 --> 00:08:26.678
I kill you myself!

00:08:30.645 --> 00:08:32.500
Battles on ice did happen.

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:33.680
There's, very famously,

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:35.603
there's the Battle on the Ice,

00:08:35.603 --> 00:08:37.830
which is a battle in which

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:39.337
the Republic of Novgorod

00:08:39.337 --> 00:08:40.840
warded off the Teutonic Knights.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:43.470
And so there was a
battle fought on the ice,

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:44.920
because, obviously, in many parts,

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.000
if you're fighting in winter,

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.110
the ice offers a nice flat space

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:49.793
where you can maneuver easily.

00:09:02.635 --> 00:09:04.500
I mean, archers would
have different ranges

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.370
based on what kind of bow they were using,

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:07.650
what kind of arrow they were using,

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:10.060
and of course their individual
experience and skill.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:11.727
Any kind of effective
missile is not going to be

00:09:11.727 --> 00:09:13.200
too much affected by the wind.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:14.753
It doesn't necessarily matter that much

00:09:14.753 --> 00:09:16.369
where they end up, as
long as they still have

00:09:16.369 --> 00:09:17.612
some force when they land.

00:09:17.612 --> 00:09:19.010
Aim for the wings of the ranks.

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:20.510
Make them cluster.

00:09:24.247 --> 00:09:25.417
I really like it, actually,

00:09:25.417 --> 00:09:27.890
that they use the arrows
to drive them together.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:30.053
So they use these arrows around the edges

00:09:30.053 --> 00:09:32.738
to drive the enemy into a narrow space,

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:35.070
where his numbers are negated.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.650
That would genuinely
have been used in battle.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.565
So we say 6 out of 10.

00:09:48.981 --> 00:09:50.560
OK, so you've got a huge line at the back,

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:52.660
and then there's another line much closer.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:55.290
So, there's, like, a
mile between those two.

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.120
What is the line in the back even doing?

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:58.270
It's going to take you 15 minutes

00:09:58.270 --> 00:09:59.812
just to walk into the battle.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.810
This is the worst example
of a Hollywood fight.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:14.270
You have big armies, but they
don't act like armies at all.

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:15.360
Slashing left and right.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:16.440
No one knows what's going on.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:17.300
There's no formations.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.590
It seems like there has
been no preparation,

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:20.520
no planning whatsoever.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:22.250
Everybody just gets together in a big mob

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:23.558
and then slams into each other.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.002
This is such a weird scene,

00:10:34.002 --> 00:10:36.170
because you see heads
being split left and right,

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:39.054
and then suddenly, when the
plot needs it to happen,

00:10:39.054 --> 00:10:40.240
there's a nice little pause,

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:41.955
and nobody will bother
her for a little bit

00:10:41.955 --> 00:10:43.930
while she mourns this guy.

00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:47.187
It's very nice of them to let her do that.

00:10:47.187 --> 00:10:48.520
If you turn away from the fighting

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.190
and drop your weapon
in this kind of melee,

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:52.350
somebody's going to shank you.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:54.143
This is obviously something
they do for plot reasons,

00:10:54.143 --> 00:10:55.470
which is totally understandable.

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:56.680
It's 1 out of 10.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:59.139
It's a complete disaster
of a depiction of battle.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:06.870
I don't want to keep
belaboring this point, but --

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:08.205
You should be digging ditches!

00:11:08.205 --> 00:11:09.110
Many ditches.

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.153
Once your first ditch is
ready, build another one.

00:11:11.153 --> 00:11:13.033
Dig many ditches.

00:11:13.033 --> 00:11:14.535
Just lots and lots of ditches.

00:11:14.535 --> 00:11:15.810
Again, where is your ditch?

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:17.370
Where is your ditch?!

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:19.140
You've got to have a ditch!

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:21.893
It shows the strength of
this kind of fortification,

00:11:21.893 --> 00:11:23.650
because the horses aren't going to try

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:24.880
and go into those ditches.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:27.545
They're going to try to
avoid that difficult terrain,

00:11:27.545 --> 00:11:30.055
and that allows you to force the enemy

00:11:30.055 --> 00:11:31.778
into where you want to fight them.

00:11:31.778 --> 00:11:34.119
So that is actually a very,
very good demonstration

00:11:34.119 --> 00:11:35.819
of how ditches are supposed to work

00:11:35.819 --> 00:11:38.512
and how a very simple
thing, like digging a pit,

00:11:38.512 --> 00:11:40.005
can actually help you enormously.

00:11:41.571 --> 00:11:44.155
Come down here! Straight through!

00:11:44.155 --> 00:11:45.405
Any way you can!

00:11:47.463 --> 00:11:48.720
This is an absolute massacre.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:50.140
This depiction of the battle,

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:51.760
it's reasonably accurate,

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:53.640
according to the accounts that survive.

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.080
So, we know that this was fought

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:57.850
in a deliberately narrow space.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:00.705
They chose a place that
was surrounded by marshes

00:12:00.705 --> 00:12:03.087
so that the enemy cavalry,
which was very strong,

00:12:03.087 --> 00:12:04.262
would have to funnel itself

00:12:04.262 --> 00:12:06.270
along one small, narrow causeway.

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:07.780
If you're a cavalry, you want flat ground,

00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:10.330
because then you can move
quickly and move freely.

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:18.150
In the movies, everybody slams together,

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:19.400
and then it's just a huge melee.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:20.380
Obviously in real life,

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:22.780
there's only a few men
who are able to fight,

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:23.961
and the rest are just kind of

00:12:23.961 --> 00:12:25.540
waiting for their turn, more or less.

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:27.913
And especially if you're in
a battle like Loudoun Hill,

00:12:27.913 --> 00:12:29.920
where you're funneled
into a narrow terrain.

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:31.840
So they chose their terrain really well.

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:33.310
And that meant that even though

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:34.830
the English had numerical advantage,

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:35.925
they couldn't use it.

00:12:35.925 --> 00:12:37.677
Retreat!

00:12:37.677 --> 00:12:39.517
Retreat!

00:12:39.517 --> 00:12:41.483
There's no winning here!

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:44.260
For this kind of battle, it is realistic,

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:45.770
in the sense that they're just shouting

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:47.580
and hoping that the men
around them will hear it

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.258
and then those men will
sort of pass it on.

00:12:50.258 --> 00:12:51.910
This is obviously not a perfect system.

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.608
So he would just hope that everybody

00:12:53.608 --> 00:12:55.900
would follow his example most of the time.

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:58.880
So it explains the
tactics, the principles,

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:00.190
but there are some other problems.

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:02.120
All the infantry has too much armor,

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:04.814
and all the horses have not enough armor.

00:13:04.814 --> 00:13:06.067
7 out of 10.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.132
So, this is Alexander's battle

00:13:17.132 --> 00:13:19.070
against the Indians at the Hydaspes,

00:13:19.070 --> 00:13:21.370
which is in modern-day Pakistan.

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:22.610
It wasn't fought in a wood,

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:24.440
it was fought on the banks of a river,

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:26.010
which Alexander had crossed in the night.

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:27.470
So, for Alexander in particular,

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:29.150
his cavalry is his trump card.

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:31.100
He wants to be able to use his cavalry.

00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:33.680
And that means he wants
to fight in open spaces.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.520
He wants to be able to
maneuver them easily,

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.410
and he wants them to
be able to run freely.

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:39.580
In a wood, there's too
much for them to trip over.

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:42.920
You can't maintain your
cavalry formation very closely.

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:44.820
Effective heavy cavalry also works

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.100
in quite tight formations.

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:48.460
Like, these horses are
shoulder to shoulder.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:50.440
If you want to use that effectively,

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:51.930
you can't obviously go through trees.

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:53.550
So he would try to avoid them.

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:55.234
All of his major battles tend to be fought

00:13:55.234 --> 00:13:57.902
on the banks of rivers or in open plains.

00:14:06.748 --> 00:14:07.639
This was the big battle

00:14:07.639 --> 00:14:09.812
where Alexander first
encountered elephants.

00:14:09.812 --> 00:14:11.295
And they're depicting this,

00:14:11.295 --> 00:14:12.320
you know, part of this, really well.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.220
Horses don't like animals they don't know.

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:15.810
You run your elephants at them,

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:17.010
and if they haven't seen them before,

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:18.940
if they haven't gotten used to each other,

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:21.680
then the horses are
going to run away first.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:23.420
You can see these elephants sort of

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:25.307
barging into these
heavy-infantry formations,

00:14:25.307 --> 00:14:26.610
and everybody's like, "What are these?

00:14:26.610 --> 00:14:27.443
How do we stop these?"

00:14:27.443 --> 00:14:31.080
But actually, Alexander won
this battle quite handily.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:32.889
The fact is that once you're
over the initial shock

00:14:32.889 --> 00:14:35.155
and you have troops that
are battle-hardened,

00:14:35.155 --> 00:14:37.180
it's quite easy to drive off elephants

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:39.290
simply by throwing missiles at them,

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:40.630
trying to get rid of their riders,

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:42.295
and panicking them so
that they'll turn around

00:14:42.295 --> 00:14:44.250
and run and trample their own men,

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:46.530
which is very often what would
happen with these elephants.

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:48.973
But at least, you know, the equipments,

00:14:48.973 --> 00:14:51.000
the tactics of the individual warriors

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:52.330
and all that is accurate.

00:14:52.330 --> 00:14:53.463
7 out of 10.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:02.150
So, hollow square is a real formation.

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:03.190
It would be used on the march.

00:15:03.190 --> 00:15:05.125
If you were afraid that

00:15:05.125 --> 00:15:07.363
the enemy might ambush
you or might attack you,

00:15:07.363 --> 00:15:09.260
they would be able to
turn at a moment's notice,

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:11.410
depending on where the threat
was going to come from.

00:15:19.071 --> 00:15:21.860
So, chariots are a feature
of mostly Bronze Age warfare.

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:23.193
They're meant to carry around

00:15:23.193 --> 00:15:24.759
people who are shooting arrows,

00:15:24.759 --> 00:15:25.840
who are throwing javelins,

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:27.670
and usually in very large numbers.

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:30.550
But as soon as you have mounted cavalry

00:15:30.550 --> 00:15:31.790
and you've mastered the art of

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:34.090
firing missiles from horseback,

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:35.610
the chariot is essentially obsolete.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:37.740
It has no real advantages over

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.270
just having lots of men on horseback,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:40.700
because they're more flexible.

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:43.370
So chariots in the course of antiquity

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:44.770
just kind of go out of style.

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:46.829
I would give it, like, 5 out of 10.

00:15:48.292 --> 00:15:49.625
Take position!

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.393
It's actually nice to see a
bit of diversity in this scene.

00:15:57.393 --> 00:15:59.040
The Roman empire obviously stretched

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:00.890
across the Mediterranean into Africa,

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:01.723
all over Europe.

00:16:01.723 --> 00:16:03.828
So you would actually
have people, you know,

00:16:03.828 --> 00:16:05.486
different skin colors,
different backgrounds.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.690
So, they dug a ditch,
and then they hid it.

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:15.020
It does happen.

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:16.510
It can be done.

00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:18.350
There is at least one
battle in Greek history

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:22.142
where the enemy has a cavalry
storming at the defenders,

00:16:22.142 --> 00:16:23.620
and the defenders have prepared the ground

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:26.070
by burying these pots in the ground,

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:27.180
just big vases,

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:28.613
and then hiding them,

00:16:28.613 --> 00:16:30.179
so that when the cavalry charges,

00:16:30.179 --> 00:16:32.970
they fall into the pots
and they break their legs.

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:34.903
So this kind of hidden trap

00:16:34.903 --> 00:16:36.480
isn't completely unheard of,

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:37.650
but it couldn't be done on this scale,

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:39.600
because you would see it. Right?

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:40.905
I mean, the effort it would take

00:16:40.905 --> 00:16:43.243
to hide a ditch on that massive scale

00:16:43.243 --> 00:16:44.320
is just incomprehensible.

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:46.326
And they would know that
these works were going on,

00:16:46.326 --> 00:16:48.507
because you didn't just
show up on that battlefield

00:16:48.507 --> 00:16:50.223
that day and started digging, right?

00:16:50.223 --> 00:16:52.245
The Roman side would
have seen them doing it.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:00.771
[laughing] Goofy scene
of a bunch of gladiators

00:17:00.771 --> 00:17:02.530
first making a nice defensive ditch

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:05.040
and then ignoring it and jumping over it.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:06.270
You've made all that effort.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:07.640
You've neutralized the enemy.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:09.080
That was your goal, right?

00:17:09.080 --> 00:17:10.590
Why did you then go back over it?

00:17:10.590 --> 00:17:11.750
If that was your plan all along,

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:13.618
why dig the ditch in the first place?

00:17:13.618 --> 00:17:16.380
It's just a silly, silly big fight.

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.170
1 out of 10.

00:17:18.170 --> 00:17:19.469
Don't run!

00:17:19.469 --> 00:17:20.969
We hold formation!

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:24.300
These are shields that are designed

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:25.460
for shield-wall formations.

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:26.920
They were even standing shields,

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:29.184
so tower shields which
are meant to be set down

00:17:29.184 --> 00:17:31.640
in battle to form a
static line of infantry.

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:33.950
Usually you would use that to set up

00:17:33.950 --> 00:17:36.080
your defensive barrier, your bulwark,

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:37.805
behind which you would then have archers,

00:17:37.805 --> 00:17:39.747
for instance, to fire at the enemy

00:17:39.747 --> 00:17:42.320
and try to get them to attack
you where you want them.

00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:44.178
So there are lots of different
cultures throughout history

00:17:44.178 --> 00:17:46.027
that have used these kind
of shield-wall tactics

00:17:46.027 --> 00:17:47.909
with very large, very heavy shields.

00:17:47.909 --> 00:17:49.340
And then in the medieval period,

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:51.890
they become more popular because
of the rise of crossbows,

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:53.208
where you start to get --

00:17:53.208 --> 00:17:55.663
your armor and your small
shields are no longer adequate.

00:17:55.663 --> 00:17:57.917
So you need these big
barriers to protect you

00:17:57.917 --> 00:17:58.883
when you're doing things like

00:17:58.883 --> 00:18:00.122
reloading your own crossbow.

00:18:00.122 --> 00:18:01.670
So you get these kind of pavises,

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:03.270
these big tower shields.

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:09.236
Fire!

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:16.900
They're setting up a
catapult, or a trebuchet.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:18.705
Like, very slowly, inevitably,

00:18:18.705 --> 00:18:20.860
because it's very heavy
and difficult to position.

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:23.150
And then you have to move these big balls,

00:18:23.150 --> 00:18:24.330
and they actually make a point of showing

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:25.490
how heavy they are.

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:28.140
You're not going to be able
to fire those very quickly.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.810
Like, it's going to have
a very slow rate of fire.

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:33.700
All that time, they're just exposed,

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:35.610
and you're standing there.

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:36.520
If you get hit by these balls,

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:37.697
you have no one to blame but yourself.

00:18:37.697 --> 00:18:39.260
[laughs]

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:40.637
It's giving you every opportunity

00:18:40.637 --> 00:18:41.690
to do something about it,

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:43.450
but instead, you're just
going to stand there.

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:45.214
Well, tactically, it's very bad.

00:18:45.214 --> 00:18:46.380
[laughs]

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:47.810
They do have some of the equipment right.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:49.258
4 out of 10.

00:18:49.258 --> 00:18:50.220
Thanks very much for watching.

00:18:50.220 --> 00:18:51.087
I hope you enjoyed that.

00:18:51.087 --> 00:18:53.450
And if you did, you can
watch my other video,

00:18:53.450 --> 00:18:54.650
which you can find here.

